at this point if you're serious about  economic issues you've probably  developed sort of a more nuanced sense  of debt and deficit and oftentimes we  hear from the right very general and  almost meaningless in how vague they are  statements or talking points like the  deficit is bad debt is bad we need to  cut in order to improve the economy cut  spending of course they will always say  when a Democrat raises the deficit that  this is what Democrats do and they will  often campaign on we lower the deficit  and we will start reducing the debt even  by creating a surplus which of course  never actually seems to end up happening  and there's four or five layers of  deception there first of all Republican  George W Bush was one of the biggest  deficit exploding presidents so first  and foremost is the question factually  do Democrats increase the deficit  whereas Republicans lower it and at  least by counterexample we know that  that's not always true Bill Clinton  actually ran a surplus george w bush  blew up the deficit of course that you  can say well clinton had the benefit of  the dot-com boom and george w bush had  the detriment of the Iraq war and 9/11  and so on and so forth but at least the  big picture of it is that it's not true  to say Democrats blow up the deficit and  Republicans reduce it but then secondly  and this is what we've been talking  about more and more on the program and  we've even done some interviews about it  but stephanie kelton and Pavlina  Chernova and others more importantly I  would say the entire paradigm that the  deficit is objectively bad has become a  political football and I would agree as  a sort of general principle that just  increasing the deficit and accruing more  debt is not a good thing in kind of a  vacuum right but going into debt for  economically stimulative programs or  reasons like food stamps which have a  very very high economic multiplier  health care for all as another example  which we don't we don't know about for  sure but has been speculated about in a  number of studies  that's much better or we could say much  less bad than going into debt to give  rich people tax cuts which will mostly  be just put in bank accounts but even  taken at its merits we also now are able  to evaluate the promises that have been  made by Donald Trump when it comes to  so-called fiscal conservatism and debt  and the deficit so for example in fiscal  year 2019 which starts in October the  White House says we will run a 1.1  trillion dollar deficit the number of  dollars of the deficit is sometimes  wrest less relevant than how big is the  deficit relative to the GDP because if  your country is growing in raw dollars  it might also be anticipated that the  deficit grows that doesn't necessarily  tell us whether it's getting bigger or  smaller as a portion of the economy we  are going to see a bigger than 5 percent  deficit as a portion of the GDP for  fiscal year 2019 under Trump okay 5  percent compared that to fiscal year  2016 for example the deficit was only  about 3 percent of GDP in fiscal year  2017  the deficit was about 3.5 percent of GDP  and it has gone up twice now under  Donald Trump Trump's first full year as  president we saw the deficit as a  percentage of GDP climbed to 4.2 percent  and then again in 2019 now it's looking  like it's going to be 5 percent of GDP  so there's a bunch of like underlying  claims here that we need to explore  first of all Donald Trump told us that  the tax cuts on the rich and on  corporations we're going to grow the  economy so much that there would be more  tax revenue brought in particularly  corporate tax cuts the corporate tax  revenues that is the opposite seems to  be true so far tax revenue to the  government from corporate taxes has been  and continues to fall so then you say ok  well fine  businesses are paying less in taxes but  they're using that tax cut that Donald  Trump gave them to pay people more to  give raises  and to hire people right well no that  that's wrong that's also not happening  as we've separately reported from other  data most of the tax cuts that  corporations have received are going to  hang out executive bonuses paying out  dividends to shareholders and buying  back their own Company stock which has  the effect of driving up the price which  of course disproportionately benefits  the shareholders who are overwhelmingly  wealthy as well as the executives so  what we get to in the end is that the  amount of the deficit in and of itself  isn't actually the problem it never  really was and maybe the right knows  this or maybe they're only hypocrites I  think that they're both a combination of  hypocrites and ignorant because we know  that they only care about the deficit  when a Democrat is president and all of  a sudden when you have george w bush  it's excuses for why the deficit went up  or just straight up lies saying the  deficit didn't go up but more  importantly to me the ways in which  Republicans like Trump raise the deficit  are bad for the economy like for example  tax cuts for the rich and tax cuts for  corporations and this to me is actually  the bigger point and the bigger  conversation I think we have to make  just deficit bad deficit good dead debt  bad debt good that's not actually the  most important conversation to have  right now because we have a more nuanced  understanding that debt can be good or  bad the deficit can be a tool used to  generate real economic growth with a  high economic multiplier or it can be a  way to let rich people sock away more  money in their bank accounts what we're  learning is that if we say hey you know  what tax cuts for less wealthy people or  healthcare for all or food stamps or  educational stimulus we can go into debt  to do those things but because of the  high economic multiplier we get more  back later on spending money now on  education might increase the deficit it  might not right but it might increase  the deficit but later because we know  more educated people commit less crime  and we know that they end up generating  more revenue  because they earn more money in the long  run because they're more educated it's  actually a net decrease on deficit that  is a much more useful way to go into  debt than just tax cuts for the rich and  then here's the last thing that I think  when it comes to Trump's claims it's  just not being accounted for when you  looked at and this is it starts to get a  little wonky but I think this is  important so let my best to explain it  clearly when we saw the analyses done of  the ten-year impact of Donald Trump's  tax reform and that's the way these are  done what's the impact over ten years  because different provisions take a  while to be put into effect and we don't  just say what will happen next year what  will happen over ten years  the calculations were based on an  economy that was going to continue doing  really well for those ten years and it's  true that the tax cuts for the rich are  cutting government revenue but if the  economy does grow some of that is going  to come back not enough to make up for  the tax cuts for the rich but at some  point there's going to be a downturn in  the economy right and as you know I  don't pretend to know exactly when it's  going to be there's a good chance in the  next ten years there's going to be a  downturn in the economy how significant  it is whether it effects the stock  market and housing and employment what  areas we don't yet know but if that  happens forget about a trillion dollar  deficit under Donald Trump you were  going to have the deficit increasing  elements of his tax plan and some of  that economic growth some of which is  real just not nearly as much as Trump  promises that's going to disappear and  there are reports talking about two or  even three trillion dollar deficits  maybe by then a Democrat will be in the  White House and Republicans can blame  them but it's really important to  understand three four or even five  layers on which the deception and the  bad policy and the the just poor  economics operate on don't fall for it  because this stuff really will rot your  mind that's when it comes to debt and  deficit and then next I want to talk  about the GDP related lies that are  being
 

